<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/development-and-inequality">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Development is not just growth. <strong>Standard of living</strong> is what money buys —
    income, goods, housing; <strong>quality of life</strong> (8.1.3) is how good living
    actually is — health, education, security, a clean environment. The two usually travel
    together but not always, and the whole discipline of development geography lives in
    that "not always". This page builds the measurement toolkit (how we compare countries
    at all), maps the uneven world the measurements reveal (and why the map looks like
    that), and weighs the strategies that have actually moved countries — trade, aid, debt
    relief, education — against their failure modes. Keep one distinction as the spine:
    <strong>the gaps run between countries <em>and</em> inside them</strong>, and the
    inside-country gaps are often the larger political fact.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring development: the indicators</h2>
  <p>
    To compare countries we need numbers (8.1.1), and they come in two families.
    <strong>Economic indicators</strong> measure the money. <em>GDP per capita</em> — the
    value of goods and services produced inside a country in a year, divided by its
    population — is the workhorse; <em>GNI per capita</em> adds what a country's residents
    earn abroad (including migrant remittances, which run over $30 billion a year into the
    Philippines and India alike) and is the UN's preferred measure. Both need two
    corrections: purchasing power parity (PPP) converts currencies by what money actually
    buys — a haircut costs less in Dhaka than in London, so PPP incomes understate
    developing-country living standards less badly — and averages hide distributions.
    <strong>Social indicators</strong> measure the life: literacy rate, life expectancy,
    infant mortality, doctors per thousand people, access to safe water, calorie intake.
    The composite <strong>Human Development Index</strong> (8.1.2) blends one from each
    family, and the classification the syllabus uses — <strong>LICs, MICs, HICs</strong>
    (low, middle, high income, by GNI per capita thresholds — roughly $1,100 and $4,500,
    adjusted each year) — is a money-only sorting that
    the social indicators constantly correct. Countries outgrow their labels slowly:
    the thresholds move, the statistics lag, and meanwhile the schools and clinics tell
    the truth.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Preston-curve shape: richer means longer-lived — but with diminishing returns (UNDP HDR 2023/24 data, 2022, rounded). Niger 1.7/62; Nigeria 6/53.5; India 9/68; Brazil 16.5/73.5; China 21.5/78.5; Japan 43/84.5" widget="plotter" config={prestonCfg} />

  <p>
    Fig. 1 is development geography's core chart. Two lessons. The <em>curve flattens</em>:
    the first few thousand dollars of GNI per person buy enormous life-expectancy gains
    (clean water, vaccines, basic clinics), the next fifty thousand buy very few — so
    "poverty kills" is literally true at the left of the chart. And the <em>scatter
    matters</em>: the USA lives about four years shorter than Japan despite far higher
    income per person; Costa Rica outlives several countries twice as rich. Income is a
    means; how a country converts it into health and schooling is the development story.
  </p>

  <h2>The HDI, computed by hand</h2>
  <p>
    The HDI combines three pillars with a geometric mean: health (life expectancy,
    rescaled from 20 to 85 years), education (mean years of schooling for adults and
    expected years for children), and income (GNI per capita PPP on a log scale, capped at
    $75,000 — the logarithm means the thousandth dollar counts for far more than the
    millionth, exactly the diminishing returns fig. 1 showed). The widget below runs the
    real UNDP formula. The defaults land near 0.75 — between the world average (0.739) and
    Brazil (0.760). Plug in Niger's figures — life expectancy about 62, roughly 2 years of
    schooling, $1,700 GNI per capita — and you get ≈ 0.40 against the published 0.394.
    Range check for 2022: Switzerland tops the table at 0.967; South Sudan sits at 0.381.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="compute a real HDI: the UNDP's own formula — watch how a log-scaled income term stops rich countries maxing out the index" widget="sliderLab" config={hdiCfg} />

  <p>
    Every indicator has limits (8.1.2), and exam answers should say them precisely.
    GDP per capita is a mean: it hides inequality (the US and Norway have similar averages;
    their income spreads are different countries), ignores the informal economy and unpaid
    care work entirely, and says nothing about what the output is (weapons or vaccines).
    The HDI adds health and education but still measures <em>capability</em>, not
    <em>conditions</em> — it is silent on rights, the environment and gender. Hence the
    alternatives: the <strong>Gini coefficient</strong> scores inequality alone (0 is
    perfect equality, 1 is one person owning everything; South Africa's ≈ 0.63 is the
    world's highest), and the <strong>Multidimensional Poverty Index</strong> counts
    deprivations directly — the UNDP's 2024 count found about 1.1 billion people poor
    across health, schooling and living standards at once, many in countries their GNI
    would call "middle income".
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> development means becoming like the West. The
    indicators are deliberately universal (health, education, income — things everyone
    values), but the <em>paths</em> are not: Japan, South Korea and Botswana developed
    differently, and culture, institutions and environment shape what a good life costs.
    Judge development by capabilities achieved, not by consumption patterns copied.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the world develops unevenly</h2>
  <p>
    Reasons (8.2.1) come in three flavours, and strong answers use them together.
    <strong>Historical-economic:</strong> colonial economies were built to extract —
    ports for exports, railways from mine to coast, taxes payable in cash crops — and
    many states inherited borders, debts and single-crop dependencies. The dependency
    persists in the numbers: UNCTAD counts more than 80 countries, mostly in Africa and
    South America, earning over 60 per cent of export income from commodities whose
    prices swing wildly — boom finances imports, bust ruins budgets (the "resource curse":
    oil-rich Nigeria has spent more decades poor than diamond-small Botswana, which
    managed its windfall into schools and infrastructure). <strong>Physical:</strong>
    being landlocked (a dozen African countries, much of the Sahel) adds a neighbour's
    politics to every export; malarial lowlands, thin soils and drought-prone climates
    raise the cost of every step; some geography is simply expensive. Geography is not
    destiny — Japan proved that on resource-poor islands — but it sets the bill.
    <strong>Social-political:</strong>
    conflict destroys in a year what decades built (South Sudan, at independence in 2011,
    fell from an HDI in the mid-0.5s to 0.381); corruption diverts the surplus; and the
    education gap — especially the schooling of girls — throttles everything downstream,
    which is why it is the single most-supported intervention in development economics.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pattern (8.2.2) is no longer the neat "north-south divide" of 1980s textbooks.
    HICs cluster in North America, western Europe, the Gulf and East Asia (Japan, South
    Korea, Singapore — plus Australia and New Zealand, south of the equator and rich);
    most of humanity lives in MICs (China, India, Brazil, Indonesia); LICs concentrate in
    Sub-Saharan Africa, with outliers in South Asia and conflict zones. The old map had
    another weakness: it implied the poor stay poor. They do not — South Korea was poorer
    than Ghana in 1960 and is now high-income; China's growth since 1978 has lifted about
    800 million people out of extreme poverty, the largest poverty reduction in history.
    And the sharpest gaps are internal: India's Kerala achieves life expectancy around 75+
    on modest income (public health spending, literacy), while its richer northern states
    lag; China's coastal provinces would rank as HICs, its western provinces as MICs.
    Development is a patchwork at every scale — which is why indicators for countries are
    only the first cut, and why the "average citizen" of any country is a statistical
    fiction worth distrust.
  </p>

  <h2>Sustainable development and the strategy toolkit</h2>
  <p>
    Sustainable development (8.3.1) is the Brundtland definition: meeting today's needs
    without compromising future generations' ability to meet theirs — growth, but within
    environmental limits and fairly shared. The UN's 17 Sustainable Development Goals
    (2015) turned it into a checklist with deadlines. Strategies (8.3.2) are social
    (education — each extra school year lifts earnings by roughly 10 per cent; vaccination
    campaigns), economic (diversification away from one crop, industrialisation,
    microfinance — Bangladesh's Grameen Bank lent tiny sums to village women and won the
    2006 Nobel Peace Prize for the experiment), and environmental (leapfrogging: Kenya
    generates about 90 per cent of its electricity from geothermal, hydro and wind, having
    never built a large fossil fleet).
  </p>
  <p>
    Evaluate the classic trio (8.3.3) with both blades. <strong>Aid</strong> — bilateral,
    multilateral or NGO — saves lives and builds schools, but tied aid (spent on the
    donor's exports) can help the giver twice, and long-run dependency can hollow out
    local accountability; emergency aid after disasters is different in kind from
    development aid. <strong>Debt relief</strong> — the HIPC initiative has cancelled more
    than $100 billion across 36 heavily indebted countries — works when the savings reach
    schools and clinics (Uganda and Tanzania's post-relief enrolment booms) and fails when
    freed borrowing capacity is spent on arms. <strong>Trade</strong> is what actually
    moved East Asia, but the terms decide who gains: sell raw coffee, capture pennies of
    the retail price; roast, brand and export, capture pounds — which is what fair-trade
    certification tries to force upward, and what industrialisation did wholesale: South
    Korea moved from wigs and textiles in the 1960s to memory chips by the 1990s, and the
    value moved with it. Drag the arrow below: exports on x, imports on
    y; a 45-degree arrow is a balanced year.
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="a year of trade for a developing country: a 45° arrow is balanced; steep means importing more than export earnings cover — the classic LIC squeeze" widget="diagramBuilder" config={tradeCfg} />

  <p>
    The detailed example (8.3.4) is <strong>Vietnam</strong>, an MIC that explains itself:
    desperately poor in 1986 when the Doi Moi reforms began, it opened to markets,
    stabilised inflation, invested ferociously in schooling and ports, and signed trade
    deals that made it a manufacturing hub — growing roughly 6-7 per cent a year for
    decades. World Bank figures show poverty falling from nearly 60 per cent in the early
    1990s to under 5 per cent today, and its HDI has climbed to 0.726. Note the recipe:
    trade plus education plus stable government — not aid — and note the trade-offs
    (environmental pressure, one-party politics) a full answer should name. Vietnam is
    also the syllabus's counterexample to every "poor countries stay poor" reflex: the
    gap that closed closed by policy, consistently applied.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert view of the inequality map is that it has split in two. <strong>Between
    countries</strong>, inequality has fallen since the 1990s: China's and India's growth
    narrowed the global gap faster than anything in economic history. <strong>Within
    countries</strong>, it has widened nearly everywhere — the top 1 per cent's share of
    income and wealth has grown in the USA, China, India and the UK alike — so the world's
    total inequality now lives mostly inside borders, where it is hardest for geography
    curricula and easiest for politics to ignore. Development measurement keeps up
    cleverly: satellite night-lights proxy for GDP where statistics are weak, mobile-money
    records map poverty street by street, and the MPI keeps poverty multidimensional. The
    unfinished debates are real: whether industrialisation can still follow East Asia's
    path in an automated world; whether "development" should mean convergence to
    high-consumption lifestyles at all (the planet-limits question that 8.3 exists to
    ask); and whether the demographic dividend — the growth burst a young workforce can
    deliver, as East Asia did — can be banked where education and jobs lag the babies.
    Hold the indicators lightly, hold the aims firmly: the tools are crude but the
    stakes are not.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Geography 0460 syllabus (2027–2029), topic 8; UNDP Human Development Report 2023/24; World Bank Open Data; UNCTAD State of Commodity Dependence; Brundtland Report, Our Common Future (1987).</p>
</TopicPage>
